* Take the contract for the Army of the North, and be paid in paper [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 2
    $ b3.war = "contract"
    $ _mp_rank = 2
  ?end
  $ standing += 2
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ do earn(14)
  $ do chart("paris.counting")
  $ do discover("the_declaration_of_war")
  You say that you will do it. You say it at the desk, in the lamplight, with a good deal less conviction than you show. It is a good contract. It will clothe, and feed, and shoe, a very great number of persons who are going to die, and it will pay, in a currency that is going to die with them, a profit that is very nearly real. Adélaïde writes the order, and sands it, and does not look up. She says that she will go to Rouen herself, for the leather. She says that it is a very fine thing to be useful, and that she has been told so, in the past, by a great many tailors.
* Put the house's reserves into gold, and send them to Amsterdam [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 1
    $ b3.war = "gold"
    $ _mp_rank = 1
  ?end
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ standing -= 1
  $ humanity -= 1
  $ do earn(-4)
  $ do chart("paris.counting")
  $ do discover("the_declaration_of_war")
  It is a matter of a night, and four letters, and a certain amount of arranging. You turn the paper into gold, at a loss, which the Bourse enjoys, and the gold into bills on Amsterdam, and the bills into a very small packet that goes out of the city at dawn in a bookseller's cart, in a bale of Voltaire. It is, as the Merchant house says, a second way. It is not, you reflect, a way that anybody would be proud of. Adélaïde, when she hears of it, says that it is a good thing that somebody in the house has the sense to be ashamed.
* Leave Collis out of it, and let the war be somebody else's ?if _mp_n == 0
  $ b3.war = "none"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ standing -= 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ do earn(-2)
  $ do chart("paris.counting")
  $ do discover("the_declaration_of_war")
  You say that you will not. You put the letter in the drawer, under the second ledger, with the Section's files, if there are any, and the Prince's card. It is a very small gesture, in the matter of forty thousand pairs of shoes, and it costs about what it ought. Étienne, who has been brought down in a chair, and sits at the foot of the table in a shawl, says, in a voice like a dry leaf, that in his experience a house is judged by what it declines. He says it to the lamp. Then he says that he is very tired.
